span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mubariz-al-din-muhammad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mubariz-al-din-muhammad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mubariz al-Din Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mubariz al-Din Muhammad was the founder of the Muzaffarid dynasty in southern Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mubariz-khan"><span class="enc-name">Mubariz Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mubariz Khan was a Mughal noble and governor of Hyderabad who was appointed by the court in Delhi to oppose the growing independence of Nizam-ul-Mulk in the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mubashshir-nasir-al-dawla"><span class="enc-name">Mubashshir Nasir al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mubashshir Nasir al-Dawla was the last ruler of the Taifa of Majorca, governing the Balearic Islands from about 1093 until his death in 1114.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/much-wenlock"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/much-wenlock.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Much Wenlock</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Much Wenlock is a market town in Shropshire, England, which grew around a monastery founded about 680 with Mildburh as its abbess.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mudaito-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Mudaito dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mudaito are an Afar clan whose dynasty ruled the Sultanate of Aussa in the lower Awash valley of north-eastern Ethiopia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mudan-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mudan-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mudan River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mudan River is a river of north-east China, about 700 kilometres long, rising in Jilin province and flowing north through Heilongjiang to join the Songhua.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mudejar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mudejar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mudéjar</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Mudéjars were Muslims who remained under Christian rule in the Iberian kingdoms as the Reconquista advanced, from the 11th to the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mudejar-revolt-of-1264-1266"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mudejar-revolt-of-1264-1266.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mudéjar revolt of 1264–1266</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1264–1266</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mudéjar revolt of 1264–1266 was an uprising of Muslims living under Castilian rule in Andalusia and Murcia, supported by the Nasrid emir of Granada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mudhoji-ii-bhonsle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mudhoji-ii-bhonsle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mudhoji II Bhonsle</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mudhoji II Bhonsle, known as Appa Sahib, was ruler of the Maratha kingdom of Nagpur from 1816 to 1818, first as regent for his cousin Parsoji, whom he is believed to…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mufarraj-ibn-sallam"><span class="enc-name">Mufarraj ibn Sallam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mufarraj ibn Sallam was the second ruler of the Emirate of Bari in southern Italy, in power in the 850s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mufarrij-ibn-daghfal"><span class="enc-name">Mufarrij ibn Daghfal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mufarrij ibn Daghfal was chief of the Banu Jarrah, a Tayyi Arab clan that dominated Palestine in the late 10th and early 11th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire"><span class="enc-name">Mughal Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mughal Empire was a dynasty of Timurid descent that ruled much of the Indian subcontinent from 1526, when Babur defeated the Lodi sultan at Panipat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mughal-war-of-succession-1657-1661"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mughal-war-of-succession-1657-1661.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mughal war of succession (1657–1661)</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1657–1661</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mughal war of succession broke out when the emperor Shah Jahan fell ill in 1657 and his four sons fought for the throne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mughal-maratha-wars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mughal-maratha-wars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mughal–Maratha Wars</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1680–1707</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mughal–Maratha Wars, also called the Deccan Wars, were fought from 1680 to 1707 between the Mughal Empire under Aurangzeb and the Maratha kingdom founded by Shivaji.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mughan-assembly"><span class="enc-name">Mughan assembly</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mughan assembly was the great gathering held on the Mughan plain in 1736 at which Nader Afshar was proclaimed shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mughan-plain"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/mughan-plain.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mughan plain</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mughan plain is a lowland steppe south of the Aras river, divided today between Azerbaijan and Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mughith-al-din-tughrilshah"><span class="enc-name">Mughith al-Din Tughrilshah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mughith al-Din Tughrilshah was a prince of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum and a son of Kilij Arslan II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mughith-umar"><span class="enc-name">al-Mughith Umar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mughith Umar was the last Ayyubid ruler of Kerak, in Transjordan, from 1250 to 1263.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abu-likaylik"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Abu Likaylik</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Abu Likaylik was a Hamaj military leader in the Funj Sultanate of Sennar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Adil Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Adil Shah was sultan of Bijapur from 1627 to 1656.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ahmad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ahmad was a Sudanese Sufi teacher who in 1881 declared himself the Mahdi and led a revolt against Turco-Egyptian rule in the Sudan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Amin Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Amin Bey, known as Lamine Bey, was the last ruler of the Husainid dynasty of Tunisia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-ayyashi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Ayyashi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Ayyashi was a Moroccan Sufi warrior who led holy war against the Spanish coastal garrisons in the early 17th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Badr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Badr was the last Zaydi imam and king of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-fasi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Fasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Fasi was a sharif, said to have come from Morocco, who around 1550 established himself in the Fezzan oases of southern Libya and founded the Awlad Muhammad…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-habib-of-trarza"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Habib of Trarza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Habib was emir of Trarza in south-western Mauritania from 1827 until 1860.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Idrisi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Idrisi was an Arab geographer and cartographer, born probably in Ceuta, who worked at the court of the Norman king Roger II of Sicily in Palermo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-khalili"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Khalili</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad bin Abdullah al-Khalili was the Ibadi Imam of Oman from 1920 until his death in 1954, ruling the interior from Nizwa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-muzaffar-of-badajoz"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Muzaffar of Badajoz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Muzaffar of Badajoz was the second Aftasid ruler of the Taifa of Badajoz, reigning from about 1045 to 1068.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Nasir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Nasir was Almohad caliph from 1199 to 1213, son of Yaqub al-Mansur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-qaim"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Qa'im</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Qa'im bi-amr Allah was the founder of the Saadi dynasty of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-sanusi-of-dar-al-kuti"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Sanusi of Dar al-Kuti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Sanusi of Dar al-Kuti was sultan of Dar al-Kuti, in what is now the north of the Central African Republic, from 1890 to 1911.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-sharif-of-wadai"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Sharif of Wadai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Sharif was sultan of Wadai from 1835 to 1858.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-shaykh-al-wattasi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Wattasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Wattasi was the founder of the Wattasid sultanate of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-tunisi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Tunisi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Tunisi was a North African scholar and traveller who lived at the court of the Darfur Sultanate from about 1803 to 1812, under Sultan Mohammed al-Fadl.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-al-abid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-al-abid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali al-Abid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali al-Abid was the first president of the Syrian Republic under the French mandate, serving from 1932 to 1936.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali Jinnah was the leader of the All-India Muslim League and the founder of Pakistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-khan-of-tonk"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali Khan of Tonk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali Khan was the Nawab of Tonk, a princely state of Rajputana in India, from 1864 to 1867.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-khan-wallajah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-khan-wallajah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah was Nawab of the Carnatic from 1749 to 1795.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali of Egypt was an Albanian officer of the Ottoman army who became governor of Egypt in 1805 and founded a dynasty that ruled until 1952.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-alwali-ii"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Alwali II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Alwali II was the last Hausa king of Kano, ruling from 1781.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-naib-of-shamil"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Amin (naib of Shamil)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Amin was an Avar religious leader sent by Imam Shamil in 1848 as his naib, or deputy, to the Circassians of the north-western Caucasus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Amin Bughra</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Amin Bughra was a Uyghur nationalist leader from Khotan in Xinjiang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-of-kazan"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Amin of Kazan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Amin of Kazan was khan of Kazan three times between 1484 and 1518.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-arsyad-al-banjari"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Arsyad al-Banjari</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Arsyad al-Banjari was an Islamic scholar from the Banjar Sultanate in southern Borneo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bahr-al-din"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Bahr al-Din</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Bahr al-Din, known as Andoka, was sultan of Dar Masalit, on the border of Sudan and Chad, from 1910 to 1951.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-baqir-majlesi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-baqir-majlesi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Baqir Majlesi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Baqir Majlesi was a leading Shia cleric of the late Safavid Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Bello</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Bello was the son of Usman dan Fodio and the first sultan of Sokoto, ruling from 1817 to 1837.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bey-abu-al-dhahab"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-bey-abu-al-dhahab.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Bey Abu al-Dhahab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Bey Abu al-Dhahab was a Mamluk commander in Ottoman Egypt and the protégé of Ali Bey al-Kabir.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-thani"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad bin Thani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad bin Thani was the leader of Doha and the first ruler of Qatar recognised by Britain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad bin Tughlaq was sultan of Delhi from 1325 to 1351, known for ambitious but unsuccessful schemes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad bin Tughluq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad bin Tughluq was sultan of Delhi from 1325 to 1351.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-daud-syah"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Daud Syah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Daud Syah was the last sultan of Aceh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-diler-khan"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Diler Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Diler Khan was an Afghan officer in Mughal service who founded the small state of Kurwai in central India in 1713.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-falihi-kaimuddin"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Falihi Kaimuddin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Falihi Kaimuddin was the last sultan of Buton, an island sultanate off south-eastern Sulawesi in Indonesia, reigning from 1937 until his death in 1960.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat was a Central Asian prince, soldier and historian of the Dughlat family and a cousin of the Mughal emperor Babur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal was a Somali politician who became prime minister of the State of Somaliland at independence in June 1960 and later prime minister of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-husayn-khan-of-derbent"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Husayn Khan of Derbent</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Husayn Khan of Derbent was the first khan of Derbent, the walled Caspian city in Dagestan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-al-mustansir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-al-mustansir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad I al-Mustansir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad I al-Mustansir was the Hafsid ruler of Ifriqiya from 1249 to 1277 and the first of the dynasty to take the title of caliph, in 1253.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-of-granada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-of-granada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad I of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad I of Granada, known as Ibn al-Ahmar, was the founder of the Nasrid dynasty and the Emirate of Granada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad I Tapar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad I Tapar was sultan of the Seljuk Empire from 1105 to 1118, son of Malik-Shah I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-aid"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn A'id</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn A'id was the last independent emir of Asir in south-western Arabia, from the Al A'id family that ruled from Abha.</span></a></li></ul>
